    "clutch": {
      "word": "CLUTCH",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "A gripe or clinching with, or as with, the fingers or claws; seizure; grasp. \"The clutch of poverty.\" Cowper. An expiring clutch at popularity. Carlyle. But Age, with his stealing steps, Hath clawed me in his clutch. Shak.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "pl.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "The hands, claws, or talons, in the act of grasping firmly; -- often figuratively, for power, rapacity, or cruelty; as, to fall into the clutches of an adversary. I must have . . . little care of myself, if I ever more come near the clutches of such a giant. Bp. Stillingfleet.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "(Mach.)",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "A device which is used for coupling shafting, etc., so as to transmit motion, and which may be disengaged at pleasure.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "Any device for gripping an object, as at the end of a chain or tackle.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "(Zoöl.)",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "The nest complement of eggs of a bird. Bayonet clutch (Mach.), a clutch in which connection is made by means of bayonets attached to arms sliding on a feathered shaft. The bayonets slide through holes in a crosshead fastened on the shaft.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "To seize, clasp, or gripe with the hand, hands, or claws; -- often figuratively; as, to clutch power. A man may set the poles together in his head, and clutch the whole globe at one intellectual grasp. Collier. Is this a dagger which I see before me . . . Come, let me clutch thee. Shak.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To close tightly; to clinch. Not that I have the power to clutch my hand. Shak.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To reach (at something) as if to grasp; to catch or snatch; -- often followed by at. Clutching at the phantoms of the stock market. Bankroft.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        }
      ],
